Boxing Legend

The circle itself (the fighter dot): blue = fighter 1 / red = fighter 2. Yellow tint = winding up a punch; grey tint = recovering after one; dark squashed grey = down on the canvas. A hurt man's whole body also rocks side to side (cosmetic).

Rings around the circle: cyan full ring plus two short cyan gloves across the front = a BLOCK; cyan half-arc = a slip (on the side the head moves); orange ring = cornered (back to two ropes); magenta/purple ring that pops and fades = a feint just completed — that's your purple circle. It means that fighter just threw a fake. Watch for it followed by the rival flashing a cyan guard against nothing — that's the rival biting on the fake.

Slate band across the front, getting thicker: he is covering up. This is a POSTURE, not an action — it thickens as he gets hurt and worn down, and it is the difference between a man boxing behind a high guard and one with his hands at his waist. A winner's stays thin all night; a man being beaten carries a thick one from the middle rounds on.

Gold ring outside everything else: he just made the other man miss and has a few ticks in which his own punch is favoured. Slipped it, and here it comes. It is rare and short, so if you see one, watch what he does with it.

Bars over the head: green→red = health, cyan-blue = stamina, thin amber = morale/belief, thin violet = how well he has read the other man, thin slate = how much he wants his hands up. The slate bar is the only one that RISES as a man does worse — health falling while it climbs is a fighter going into his shell.

On the ring: gold line = the clinch (above); big white number = the referee's knockdown count.